-- title:
--   Germ of varpi generates a non-zero prime in each stalk on the special fibre
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative domain and $\varpi\in R$ a non-zero element such that the ideal $(\varpi)=\mathrm{span}\{\varpi\}$ is maximal; let $\kappa$ be a field and $q\colon R\to\kappa$ a ring homomorphism whose kernel is exactly $(\varpi)$ (so $R/(\varpi)$ embeds in $\kappa$, $q$ not being required to be surjective). Let $c\colon X\to\operatorname{Spec} R$ be a morphism of schemes with $X$ integral and $c$ smooth of relative dimension $1$, and assume moreover that the fibre product $X\times_{\operatorname{Spec} R}\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$, formed along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $q$, is integral. Let $x$ be a point of $X$ lying in the image of the first projection of that fibre product, i.e. a point of the special fibre. Write $\varpi_x\in\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ for the germ at $x$ of the global section of $X$ obtained from $\varpi$ by transporting it along the isomorphism $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} R)\cong R$ and then applying $c$ on global sections. The conclusion is the conjunction: the principal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ generated by $\varpi_x$ is prime (in particular proper), and $\varpi_x\neq 0$.
--
--   This is the local form of the statement that a smooth relative curve over $R$ whose special fibre over $(\varpi)$ is integral has, at each point of that fibre, a reduced irreducible branch of the special fibre cut out by $\varpi$ alone. It is used in the construction of cusp parameters on modular curves over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, where it supplies the discrete-valuation-type input to the stalk computations at a cusp.
